### What are Java Records?

A record is an immutable data carrier declared in one line. The compiler generates a canonical constructor, private final fields, public accessor methods (named after the components, not getX()), and value-based equals, hashCode, and toString. Records are implicitly final and cannot extend a class, but they can implement interfaces.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// One line replaces 50+ lines of POJO boilerplate
public record TransferRequest(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {}
```

The compiler generates:
- `private final String fromAccount`, `private final String toAccount`, `private final BigDecimal amount`
- `TransferRequest(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount)` constructor
- `fromAccount()`, `toAccount()`, `amount()` accessor methods (NOT getFromAccount)
- `equals()` comparing all components
- `hashCode()` based on all components
- `toString()` showing all components

### How do you validate with a compact constructor?

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record TransferRequest(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
    // Compact constructor: no parameter list, no field assignments
    TransferRequest {
        if (fromAccount == null || fromAccount.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("From account is required");
        }
        if (amount.signum() <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        }
        // Field assignments happen automatically AFTER this block
    }
}
```

```java
// Usage:
new TransferRequest("SAV2001", "SAV2002", new BigDecimal("50000")); // OK
new TransferRequest("", "SAV2002", new BigDecimal("50000"));        // throws IAE
new TransferRequest("SAV2001", "SAV2002", BigDecimal.ZERO);         // throws IAE
```

### How do records compare to regular classes?

| Feature | Record | Regular Class |
|---------|--------|---------------|
| Boilerplate | 1 line | 50+ lines |
| Mutability | Immutable (final fields) | Mutable (setters) |
| equals/hashCode | Auto (all components) | Manual or IDE-generated |
| toString | Auto (all components) | Manual |
| Inheritance | Cannot extend classes | Can extend |
| Accessor naming | component() | getComponent() |
| Serializable | Yes (if components are) | Requires explicit |

### How do you use records with streams?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RecordStreamDemo {
    record Trade(String symbol, String side, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Trade> trades = List.of(
            new Trade("RELIANCE", "BUY", new BigDecimal("45000")),
            new Trade("TCS", "SELL", new BigDecimal("78000")),
            new Trade("RELIANCE", "SELL", new BigDecimal("32000"))
        );

        // Records work perfectly as stream elements
        Map<String, BigDecimal> totalBySymbol = trades.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Trade::symbol,
                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Trade::amount, BigDecimal::add)));

        System.out.println(totalBySymbol);
        // {RELIANCE=77000, TCS=78000}

        // Records make excellent Map keys because equals/hashCode are correct
        Map<Trade, String> tradeStatus = Map.of(
            new Trade("RELIANCE", "BUY", new BigDecimal("45000")), "SETTLED");

        // Two records with same components are equal
        Trade lookup = new Trade("RELIANCE", "BUY", new BigDecimal("45000"));
        System.out.println(tradeStatus.get(lookup)); // SETTLED
    }
}
```

### What can records NOT do?

```java
// CANNOT extend a class (implicitly extends Record)
// public record Trade(...) extends BaseEntity {} // COMPILE ERROR

// CANNOT add mutable instance fields
// public record Trade(String id) { int counter = 0; } // COMPILE ERROR

// CAN implement interfaces
public record Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount) implements Serializable {}

// CAN add static fields and methods
public record Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
    public static final Trade EMPTY = new Trade("", BigDecimal.ZERO);
    public static Trade of(String id, String amount) {
        return new Trade(id, new BigDecimal(amount));
    }
}

// CAN add instance methods
public record Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
    public boolean isLarge() { return amount.compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0; }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: expecting getter naming convention
Trade t = new Trade("TRD-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
// t.getId();     // COMPILE ERROR: no getId()
t.id();           // CORRECT: component name, not JavaBean style

// WRONG: trying to modify a record
// t.setAmount(new BigDecimal("60000")); // records have no setters
// CORRECT: create a new record
Trade updated = new Trade(t.id(), new BigDecimal("60000"));

// WRONG: assuming equals ignores BigDecimal scale
Trade t1 = new Trade("A", new BigDecimal("100"));
Trade t2 = new Trade("A", new BigDecimal("100.00"));
System.out.println(t1.equals(t2)); // false! BigDecimal.equals checks scale
```

### What about record patterns (Java 21)?

```java
// Deconstruct records in instanceof and switch
Object obj = new Trade("TRD-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));

if (obj instanceof Trade(String id, BigDecimal amount)) {
    System.out.println("Trade " + id + ": Rs." + amount);
}

// Switch with record patterns
String describe(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Trade(String id, BigDecimal amt) when amt.compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0
            -> "Large trade: " + id;
        case Trade(String id, BigDecimal amt)
            -> "Trade: " + id;
        default -> "Unknown";
    };
}
```

### When should you use records vs classes?

| Use Case | Record | Class |
|----------|--------|-------|
| DTO / API response | Yes | No |
| Value object | Yes | No |
| Map key | Yes (correct equals) | Risky (manual equals) |
| Domain entity with behavior | No | Yes |
| Mutable state needed | No | Yes |
| Inheritance hierarchy | No | Yes |
| JPA entity | Caution (no no-arg constructor) | Yes |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Records + sealed classes + pattern matching = modern Java's algebraic data types.
- Use records for DTOs, value objects, events, and any class whose identity is its data.
- BigDecimal.equals checks scale, so `new BigDecimal("100").equals(new BigDecimal("100.00"))` is false. Use compareTo for value equality in records containing BigDecimal.
- Records are thread-safe because all fields are final. Safe to share across threads without synchronization.
- JPA entities require a no-arg constructor, so records do not work directly as JPA entities. Use records for DTOs and projections instead.
